4441	79Z2VNV3	\N	Zum variationslinguistischen Verhältnis von Stadt und Land.: Ein Beitrag zu einer sprachlichen Raumtopologie am Beispiel Österreichs	{"key": "79Z2VNV3", "data": {"DOI": "10.13092/lo.110.8147", "key": "79Z2VNV3", "url": "https://bop.unibe.ch/linguistik-online/article/view/8147", "ISSN": "1615-3014", "PMID": "", "date": "2021-10-19", "tags": [], "PMCID": "", "extra": "", "issue": "5", "pages": "293-329", "place": "", "title": "Zum variationslinguistischen Verhältnis von Stadt und Land.: Ein Beitrag zu einer sprachlichen Raumtopologie am Beispiel Österreichs", "rights": "", "series": "", "volume": "110", "archive": "", "section": "", "version": 1991, "creators": [{"lastName": "Pickl", "firstName": "Simon", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Pröll", "firstName": "Simon", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Elspaß", "firstName": "Stephan", "creatorType": "author"}], "itemType": "journalArticle", "language": "", "dateAdded": "2026-06-01T07:27:20Z", "partTitle": "", "publisher": "", "relations": {}, "accessDate": "2026-06-01T07:27:20Z", "callNumber": "", "partNumber": "", "seriesText": "", "shortTitle": "Zum variationslinguistischen Verhältnis von Stadt und Land.", "citationKey": "", "collections": ["6UQ4N3F4"], "seriesTitle": "", "abstractNote": "In this paper, we explore the geolinguistic relationship between urban and rural areas through the conceptualisation and modeling of spatial topologies. Geolinguistic topologies concern the structure of the mutual linguistic relationship between localities. They can be defined either deductively or on the basis of empirical data and represent the linguistic similarities or distances between localities. We operationalise and apply several such topological models to Austrian data from the Atlas zur deutschen Alltagssprache (AdA), a linguistic atlas documenting colloquial German using crowd-sourcing methods. The results are evaluated on the basis of statistical examination and of visualisations of the topological relationships predicted by the models. It is confirmed that linguistic similarity is determined both by geographical distance and by the distribution of population, but the exact relationship is complex: Not only do smaller geographic distances on the one hand and higher population numbers on the other hand bring about increased linguistic similarity; the relevance of these two factors for linguistic similarity varies with population size, too, such that linguistic relationships between cities are determined more by their size and less by their distance, while for smaller locations the opposite is true. Hence, no single topological model can be identified as superior; instead, the individual models emphasise different aspects of the linguistic relationship between urban and regional language usage.", "dateModified": "2026-06-01T07:27:20Z", "libraryCatalog": "DOI.org (Crossref)", "archiveLocation": "", "publicationTitle": "Linguistik Online", "journalAbbreviation": "LO"}, "meta": {"parsedDate": "2021-10-19", "numChildren": 0, "createdByUser": {"id": 7831302, "name": "", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/lumpazi", "type": "text/html"}}, "username": "Lumpazi"}, "creatorSummary": "Pickl et al."}, "links": {"self": {"href": "https://api.zotero.org/groups/5540614/items/79Z2VNV3", "type": "application/json"}, "alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform/items/79Z2VNV3", "type": "text/html"}}, "library": {"id": 5540614, "name": "LexAT_Sprachplattform", "type": "group", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform", "type": "text/html"}}}, "version": 1991}
4442	78UT6RJW	\N	Reconciling the social and spatial: An apparent-time analysis of variation intensity in colloquial German	{"key": "78UT6RJW", "data": {"DOI": "10.1017/jlg.2025.10001", "key": "78UT6RJW", "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-linguistic-geography/article/reconciling-the-social-and-spatial-an-apparenttime-analysis-of-variation-intensity-in-colloquial-german/178686FC7E807D490F57AD727515C261", "ISSN": "2049-7547", "PMID": "", "date": "2025/04", "tags": [{"tag": "Atlas zur deutschen Alltagssprache", "type": 1}, {"tag": "apparent-time analysis", "type": 1}, {"tag": "ergodicity", "type": 1}, {"tag": "language change", "type": 1}, {"tag": "variation intensity", "type": 1}], "PMCID": "", "extra": "", "issue": "1", "pages": "57-70", "place": "", "title": "Reconciling the social and spatial: An apparent-time analysis of variation intensity in colloquial German", "rights": "", "series": "", "volume": "13", "archive": "", "section": "", "version": 1990, "creators": [{"lastName": "Wirtz", "firstName": "Mason A.", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Pickl", "firstName": "Simon", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Niehaus", "firstName": "Konstantin", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Elspaß", "firstName": "Stephan", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Möller", "firstName": "Robert", "creatorType": "author"}], "itemType": "journalArticle", "language": "en", "dateAdded": "2026-06-01T07:25:00Z", "partTitle": "", "publisher": "", "relations": {}, "accessDate": "2026-06-01T07:24:57Z", "callNumber": "", "partNumber": "", "seriesText": "", "shortTitle": "Reconciling the social and spatial", "citationKey": "", "collections": ["6UQ4N3F4"], "seriesTitle": "", "abstractNote": "In this article, we aim to reconcile the interrelated role of social and spatial influences on colloquial German by conducting an apparent-time analysis of 15 lexical, morphological, and phonetic variables from the Atlas of Colloquial German (AdA). Specifically, we introduce a “change in variation intensity” (CVI) measure meant to gauge the degree of change in colloquial speech between younger and older cohorts. Using this measure, we investigate (a) whether the CVI within and across variables is prone to spatial patterns and (b) the extent to which the CVI differs systematically between variable types. Results from visual–spatial analyses indicate clear spatial patterns, though the geographical distributions are largely item-specific, and a linear mixed-effects model revealed no effect of variable type. More broadly, the CVI measure represents an important advancement in how we address language variation and change from a methodological-statistical perspective, specifically when dealing with heterogeneous, crowdsourced data.", "dateModified": "2026-06-01T07:25:00Z", "libraryCatalog": "Cambridge University Press", "archiveLocation": "", "publicationTitle": "Journal of Linguistic Geography", "journalAbbreviation": ""}, "meta": {"parsedDate": "2025-04", "numChildren": 0, "createdByUser": {"id": 7831302, "name": "", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/lumpazi", "type": "text/html"}}, "username": "Lumpazi"}, "creatorSummary": "Wirtz et al."}, "links": {"self": {"href": "https://api.zotero.org/groups/5540614/items/78UT6RJW", "type": "application/json"}, "alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform/items/78UT6RJW", "type": "text/html"}}, "library": {"id": 5540614, "name": "LexAT_Sprachplattform", "type": "group", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform", "type": "text/html"}}}, "version": 1990}
4443	SRZWDYFG	\N	Analyzing linguistic variation and change using gamification web apps: The case of German-speaking Europe	{"key": "SRZWDYFG", "data": {"DOI": "10.1371/journal.pone.0225399", "key": "SRZWDYFG", "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0225399", "ISSN": "1932-6203", "PMID": "", "date": "11.12.2019", "tags": [{"tag": "Austria", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Dialectology", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Europe", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Germany", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Language", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Metadata", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Sports", "type": 1}, {"tag": "Switzerland", "type": 1}], "PMCID": "", "extra": "", "issue": "12", "pages": "e0225399", "place": "", "title": "Analyzing linguistic variation and change using gamification web apps: The case of German-speaking Europe", "rights": "", "series": "", "volume": "14", "archive": "", "section": "", "version": 1989, "creators": [{"lastName": "Leemann", "firstName": "Adrian", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Derungs", "firstName": "Curdin", "creatorType": "author"}, {"lastName": "Elspaß", "firstName": "Stephan", "creatorType": "author"}], "itemType": "journalArticle", "language": "en", "dateAdded": "2026-06-01T07:22:00Z", "partTitle": "", "publisher": "", "relations": {}, "accessDate": "2026-06-01T07:21:58Z", "callNumber": "", "partNumber": "", "seriesText": "", "shortTitle": "Analyzing linguistic variation and change using gamification web apps", "citationKey": "", "collections": ["6UQ4N3F4"], "seriesTitle": "", "abstractNote": "Research on regional linguistic variation typically involves data collection in the field. This process itself can take up several months if not years. In the present study we demonstrate how we can use web interactives in collaboration with media outlets for a fast gathering of regional, sociolinguistic data. In collaboration with SPIEGEL ONLINE and Tagesanzeiger, we developed a web interactive that predicts users’ regional backgrounds from within German-speaking Europe. More than 1.9M people have participated in the interactive, more than 770K users have provided metadata. Said metadata allowed us to capture regional variation in language as of today, which we can compare to historical survey data–enabling us to track the evolution of German in Europe over the past 40 years. We report on regional levelling of lexical variants, a process which appears to be particularly prevalent in the northern parts of German-speaking Europe. We further found an effect of (former) national and regional borders on language use. This innovative paradigm allows us to collect sociolinguistic data of an unprecedented scale–at the same time it presents significant challenges, both of which–benefits and challenges–will be discussed in this contribution.", "dateModified": "2026-06-01T07:22:00Z", "libraryCatalog": "PLoS Journals", "archiveLocation": "", "publicationTitle": "PLOS ONE", "journalAbbreviation": "PLOS ONE"}, "meta": {"parsedDate": "2019-11-12", "numChildren": 0, "createdByUser": {"id": 7831302, "name": "", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/lumpazi", "type": "text/html"}}, "username": "Lumpazi"}, "creatorSummary": "Leemann et al."}, "links": {"self": {"href": "https://api.zotero.org/groups/5540614/items/SRZWDYFG", "type": "application/json"}, "alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform/items/SRZWDYFG", "type": "text/html"}}, "library": {"id": 5540614, "name": "LexAT_Sprachplattform", "type": "group", "links": {"alternate": {"href": "https://www.zotero.org/groups/lexat_sprachplattform", "type": "text/html"}}}, "version": 1989}
